WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.365
Module Five, Lesson Three: the four types of memory.

2
00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:06.930
The context window is working memory, and it is not enough.

3
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.008
Picture a running coach agent that chats with Maya every week.

4
00:00:11.338 --> 00:00:15.704
Every week she repeats that she's vegetarian and training for a half marathon.

5
00:00:16.504 --> 00:00:19.889
It has also forgotten the knee injury that stalled her last spring.

6
00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:24.350
Agents need memory that outlasts the session, and it comes in four types.

7
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.459
Working memory is the context window:

8
00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:30.519
the goal, recent turns, the latest observations.

9
00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:33.060
It is always there, because it is the prompt.

10
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.290
Semantic memory holds durable facts and preferences, like vegetarian.

11
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.709
It lives in a vector or key-value store, and is retrieved when relevant.

12
00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:48.088
Episodic memory records specific past events and outcomes, like that injury.

13
00:00:48.418 --> 00:00:51.589
It is recalled when a similar situation comes up again.

14
00:00:52.389 --> 00:00:54.507
Procedural memory stores learned how-to,

15
00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:57.054
like the tool sequence that builds a good plan.

16
00:00:57.384 --> 00:01:01.153
It sits in a skill registry, and is used when the task type matches.

17
00:01:01.953 --> 00:01:06.383
Writing is the hard part, because saving every turn fills the store with noise.

18
00:01:06.713 --> 00:01:10.588
A write policy keeps only durable facts, and deduplicates them.

19
00:01:11.388 --> 00:01:14.538
When Maya goes vegan, the new fact contradicts the old one.

20
00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.542
Supersede it with a timestamp,

21
00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:19.874
instead of keeping both and letting retrieval flip a coin.

22
00:01:20.674 --> 00:01:25.936
Forgetting is a feature: age out unused entries, and let unreinforced facts decay.

23
00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:30.077
Memory that only grows gets slower, costlier, and less accurate.

24
00:01:30.877 --> 00:01:34.326
If Maya asks to be erased, that includes the agent's memory.

25
00:01:34.656 --> 00:01:39.086
So memory is partitioned by user, with hard isolation on every read.

26
00:01:39.886 --> 00:01:42.182
Memory is also an injection surface:

27
00:01:42.232 --> 00:01:45.767
text planted today can fire as an instruction tomorrow.

28
00:01:46.097 --> 00:01:49.245
Sanitize on write, treat it as untrusted data on read,

29
00:01:49.295 --> 00:01:51.615
and never let it grant tool permissions.

30
00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:55.639
Inside one long run, summaries replace old turns,

31
00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:58.445
and big tool results become short digests.

32
00:01:58.775 --> 00:02:01.370
Key evidence goes at the start and end of the prompt.

33
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.307
On the exam, expect to place a need in one type:

34
00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:09.224
the customer's plan is semantic, a past outage is episodic.

35
00:02:09.554 --> 00:02:13.877
A reusable query pattern is procedural, and the lesson below has the rest.
